// Reference snippet: anti-collision label placement for multiple price levels.
//
// NOTE: Pine Script has no local file imports — you CANNOT `import` this file.
// Paste the spreadLabels() function and the usage pattern into your script.
//
// pine-lint-disable PINE001,PINE002,PINE022,PINE027,PINE051,PINE056
// (Fragment file — no indicator() declaration; helpers intentionally uncalled.)

// ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
// THE PROBLEM:
//   When multiple price levels cluster near the same price (e.g. PDH next
//   to a swing high, or CDL next to PDL), their labels stack on top of each
//   other. The result is a single pile of overlapping text that reads as
//   garbage. This is the most common visual defect in multi-level indicators.
//
// THE SOLUTION:
//   Sort labels by price (descending), walk the list, and push each label
//   DOWN when it would be closer than `minGap` to the previous one.
//   The LINE stays at the true price — only the LABEL TEXT moves.
//   Use a fraction of ATR as minGap so it scales with the instrument.
//
// IMPORTANT: call this only inside `if barstate.islast`. It processes the
// entire array on every realtime tick — which is fine because the array is
// small (~3–10 items) and `islast` does not re-run on historical bars.
// ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

// @function Computes anti-collision Y positions for a set of price labels.
//
// Takes an array of TRUE prices and returns an array of ADJUSTED Y positions
// with the same indices. Labels that would overlap are nudged downward until
// there is at least `minGap` between adjacent label centers.
//
// @param prices    array<float>  The true price for each level (parallel to your label array).
// @param atrValue  float         Current ATR — minGap is derived as atrValue * gapFactor.
// @param gapFactor float         Fraction of ATR between label centers (default 0.35).
// @returns         array<float>  Adjusted Y positions (same indices as input).
//
// Usage:
//   Only the LABEL Y changes — your LINE stays at the true price.
//   Example:
//     array<float> adjusted = spreadLabels(levelPrices, ta.atr(14), 0.35)
//     for i = 0 to array.size(levelPrices) - 1
//         float truePrice    = array.get(levelPrices, i)
//         float labelY       = array.get(adjusted,    i)
//         label lbl          = array.get(labelArray,  i)
//         label.set_y(lbl, labelY)
//         // line stays at truePrice — already drawn

spreadLabels(array<float> prices, float atrValue, float gapFactor) =>
    int           n        = array.size(prices)
    array<float>  adjusted = array.copy(prices)          // output — same indices
    float         minGap   = atrValue * math.max(gapFactor, 0.05)

    if n > 0
        // Sort indices descending by price (highest label placed first)
        array<int> order = array.sort_indices(prices, order.descending)
        float      lastY = na

        for k = 0 to n - 1
            int   i      = array.get(order, k)
            float p      = array.get(prices, i)
            float labelY = p

            // If this label would be too close to the previous one, push it down.
            if not na(lastY) and (lastY - labelY) < minGap
                labelY := lastY - minGap

            lastY := labelY
            array.set(adjusted, i, labelY)

    adjusted


// ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
